  ($q$alg-540$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear. If f(3) = −4 and f(6) = −7, what is the value of f(1)?$q$, $q$["−2","−3","−1","0"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The slope is (−7 − (−4))/(6 − 3) = −1, and f(0) = −1. So f(1) = −1(1) − 1 = −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-541$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A taxi company charges $6.00 for the first mile and $1.00 for each additional mile. A passenger paid $19.00 for a ride. How many miles was the ride? (Assume the ride covered a whole number of miles.)$q$, $q$["19","12","15","14"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first mile costs $6.00, leaving $19.00 − $6.00 = $13.00 for additional miles: 13 additional miles at $1.00. Total distance = 1 + 13 = 14 miles.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-542$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$2x + 6y = 13 kx + 12y = 41 In the system above, k is a constant. For what value of k does the system have no solution?$q$, $q$["8","2","12","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$No solution requires parallel lines: the ratios of x- and y-coefficients match but the constants do not. k/2 = 12/6, so k = 4. (The constants 13 and 41 are not in that same ratio, so the lines are distinct.)$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-543$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A ticket office sold 84 adult and student tickets for a school play in one day. Adult tickets cost $10 each, and student tickets cost $5 each. If the total revenue was $585, how many adult tickets were sold?$q$, $q$["28","33","51","74"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let a be the number of adult tickets. Then a + s = 84 and 10a + 5s = 585. Substituting s = 84 − a gives 5a = 165, so a = 33.$q$, null),
