  ($q$adv-763$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 2, null, $q$How many distinct real solutions does the equation 2x² − x − 3 = 0 have?$q$, $q$["No real solutions","Infinitely many solutions","Exactly two distinct real solutions","Exactly one real solution"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The discriminant is b² − 4ac = (−1)² − 4(2)(−3) = 25. A positive discriminant means two distinct real solutions.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-764$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 2, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (6x + 5)²?$q$, $q$["36x² + 30x + 25","36x² + 60x + 25","6x² + 60x + 25","36x² + 25"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$(p + q)² = p² + 2pq + q². With p = 6x and q = 5: 36x² + 2(6x)(5) + 5² = 36x² + 60x + 25.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-765$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 1, null, $q$The function f(t) = 2400(1.05)^t models the population of a town t years after it was first measured. What is the best interpretation of 2400 in this context?$q$, $q$["The amount when it was first measured","The amount after one year","The change in the amount each year","The amount after 1.05 years"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When t = 0, f(0) = 2400(1.05)^0 = 2400. So 2400 is the amount at the time of the first measurement.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-766$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f(t) = 1200(1.05)^t gives the number of fish in a lake t months after the start of a study. Which statement best describes how f changes?$q$, $q$["It increases by 5 each month.","It increases by 5% each month.","It decreases by 5% each month.","It increases by 1.05% each month."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In f(t) = a(b)^t, the base b = 1.05 is 1 + 0.05, so f is multiplied by 1.05 each month. That is a 5% increase per month.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-767$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = −2x² + 24x − 67. What is the maximum value of f?$q$, $q$["−67","5","6","−77"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The vertex occurs at x = −b/(2a) = −24/−4 = 6. Then f(6) = 5. Because the parabola opens downward, the maximum value is 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-768$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 1, null, $q$What are the solutions to the equation (x − 5)(x + 5) = 0?$q$, $q$["x = −5 and x = −5","x = 5 and x = 5","x = 5 and x = −5","x = −5 and x = 5"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A product equals 0 when one of its factors equals 0. Setting (x − 5) = 0 and (x + 5) = 0 gives x = 5 and x = −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-769$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, null, $q$For the exponential function f, f(0) = 4 and f(1) = 12. What is the value of f(3)?$q$, $q$["36","28","1728","108"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$f(x) = a·b^x with a = f(0) = 4. Since f(1) = 4·b = 12, b = 3. So f(3) = 4·3^3 = 108.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-770$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 1, null, $q$The function f(t) = 1200(2)^t models the value, in dollars, of a collectible card t years after it was first measured. What is the best interpretation of 1200 in this context?$q$, $q$["The amount when it was first measured","The amount after 2 years","The amount after one year","The change in the amount each year"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When t = 0, f(0) = 1200(2)^0 = 1200. So 1200 is the amount at the time of the first measurement.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-771$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the solution to the equation √(4x + 8) = 6?$q$, $q$["7","11","9","−1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Square both sides: 4x + 8 = 36. Subtract 8 from both sides to get 4x = 28, so x = 7. Checking, √(36) = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-772$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 1, null, $q$What are the solutions to the equation −(x + 1)(x − 9) = 0?$q$, $q$["x = −1 and x = −9","x = −1 and x = 9","x = 1 and x = −9","x = 1 and x = 9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A product equals 0 when one of its factors equals 0. Setting (x + 1) = 0 and (x − 9) = 0 gives x = −1 and x = 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-773$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, null, $q$For the exponential function f, f(0) = 5 and f(1) = 20. What is the value of f(3)?$q$, $q$["320","80","50","60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$f(x) = a·b^x with a = f(0) = 5. Since f(1) = 5·b = 20, b = 4. So f(3) = 5·4^3 = 320.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-774$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 1, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (8x⁵)(5x⁵)?$q$, $q$["13x²⁵","13x¹⁰","40x¹⁰","40x²⁵"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Multiply the coefficients (8 × 5 = 40) and add the exponents of x (5 + 5 = 10).$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-775$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 1, null, $q$The function p is defined by p(x) = 2x³ + 5x² − x + 2. What is the value of p(−2)?$q$, $q$["8","4","2","36"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute −2 for x and evaluate each term: p(−2) = −16 + 20 + 2 + 2 = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-776$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 1, null, $q$The function p is defined by p(x) = −4x³ + 2x² + 4x − 7. What is the value of p(−3)?$q$, $q$["−7","−85","121","107"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Substitute −3 for x and evaluate each term: p(−3) = 108 + 18 − 12 − 7 = 107.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-777$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 1, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (4x²)⁴?$q$, $q$["256x⁶","16x⁸","256x⁸","4x⁸"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Raise each factor to the power 4: 4^4 = 256, and (x^2)^4 = x^8.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-778$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 16^(x + 1) = 2^(6x), what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["2","2/5","1/5","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write 16 as 2^4: 2^(4(x + 1)) = 2^(6x). Equate exponents: 4x + 4 = 6x, so 2x = 4 and x = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-779$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the value of 81^(1/2)?$q$, $q$["81/2","9","−9","1/9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$81^(1/2) = (the square root of 81)^1 = 9^1 = 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-780$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 2, null, $q$y = x² + 3x + 1<br>y = 2x + 1<br><br>The graphs of the equations above intersect at two points. What is the product of the x-coordinates of the intersection points?$q$, $q$["2","1","0","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Set the expressions equal: x² + 3x + 1 = 2x + 1, so x² + x = 0. This factors as x(x + 1) = 0, so x = 0 or x = −1. The product is 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-781$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the solution to the equation √(x − 5) = 2?$q$, $q$["9","4","−1","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Square both sides: x − 5 = 4. Add 5 to both sides to get x = 9, so x = 9. Checking, √(4) = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-782$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = (x + 1)² − 7. The graph of y = f(x) in the xy-plane has its vertex at (h, k). What is the value of h + k?$q$, $q$["−6","−8","6","8"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In vertex form a(x − h)² + k, the vertex is (h, k). Here the vertex is (−1, −7), so h + k = −8.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-783$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 1, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (3x³)⁴?$q$, $q$["3x¹²","81x⁷","12x¹²","81x¹²"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Raise each factor to the power 4: 3^4 = 81, and (x^3)^4 = x^12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −2x² − 19x − 5<br>y = x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−5","−45","43","45"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: −2x² − 19x − 5 = x + k, so −2x² − 20x − 5 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (−20)² − 4(−2)(−5 − k) = 0, which gives k = 45. The repeated solution is x = −(−20)/(2·(−2)) = −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(3x + 16) = x + 4<br><br>What is the solution to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−5","5","0","1"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 3x + 16 = x² + 8x + 16, so x² + 5x = 0, which factors as x(x + 5) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 4 must be nonnegative. For x = −5, the right side is −1 < 0, so x = −5 is extraneous. Only x = 0 works.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$3<sup>2x</sup> − 108(3<sup>x</sup>) + 2187 = 0<br><br>What is the sum of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["108","7","12","81"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 3<sup>x</sup>, so 3<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 108u + 2187 = 0, which factors as (u − 27)(u − 81) = 0. So 3<sup>x</sup> = 27 or 3<sup>x</sup> = 81, giving x = 3 or x = 4. The sum is 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + ax² + bx − 30<br><br>In the given polynomial function, a and b are constants. If −1 and 5 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["−6","6","−30","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + d, the product of the three zeros is −d. Here −d = 30, so (−1)(5)r = 30, giving −5r = 30 and r = −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 72t + 38. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 110 feet?$q$, $q$["3","2.25","0.75","1.5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 110: −16t² + 72t + 38 = 110, so −16t² + 72t − 72 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 4.5t + 4.5 = 0, so t = 1.5 or t = 3. The object is at or above 110 feet between these times, for 3 − 1.5 = 1.5 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$A population of bacteria increases by 96% every 2 weeks. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per week, by what percent does it increase each week?$q$, $q$["48%","96%","40%","24%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-week growth factor is r, then r<sup>2</sup> = 1 + 0.96 = 1.96. Taking the square root gives r = 1.4, which is a 40% increase each week. Dividing 96% by 2 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(1) = 54 and f(3) = 216, what is the value of f(2)?$q$, $q$["135","108","54","216"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(3)/f(1) = b<sup>2</sup> = 4, so b = 2. Then a = f(1)/b<sup>1</sup> = 27, so f(x) = 27(2)<sup>x</sup> and f(2) = 27(2)<sup>2</sup> = 108.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + 8x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 4. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["12","15","16","−12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to −8 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 4. So they are (−8 + 4)/2 = −2 and (−8 − 4)/2 = −6. Their product is c = (−2)(−6) = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + kx + 10 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is an integer constant. For how many values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["6","15","12","13"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$No real solutions means the discriminant is negative: k² − 4(1)(10) < 0, so k² < 40. The integers satisfying this are −6 through 6, including 0, which is 13 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 3, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to √(x) · <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>5</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>−1/3</sup>","x<sup>5/12</sup>","x<sup>4/3</sup>","x<sup>3/4</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: √(x) = x<sup>1/2</sup> and <sup>6</sup>√(x<sup>5</sup>) = x<sup>5/6</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 1/2 + 5/6 = 4/3, so the expression equals x<sup>4/3</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$(x² + kx + 35)/(x − 7) = x − 5<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant, and the equation is true for all x ≠ 7. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−2","−12","2","35"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by x − 7 gives x² + kx + 35 = (x − 7)(x − 5) = x² − 12x + 35. Matching x-coefficients gives k = −12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = −5. If f(0) = −22 and f(−6) = 2, what is the value of f(−4)?$q$, $q$["2","3","1","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − (−5))² + k. From f(0): a(25) + k = −22. From f(−6): a(1) + k = 2. Subtracting gives 24a = −24, so a = −1 and k = 3. Then f(−4) = −1(1)² + 3 = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = 3x³ + ax² − 2x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x + 3, the remainder is −91, and p(1) = −7. What is the value of ab?$q$, $q$["−8","−7","−1","7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(−3) = −91: −81 + 9a + 6 + b = −91, so 9a + b = −16. Also p(1) = −7: a + b = −8. Subtracting, 8a = −8, so a = −1 and b = −7. Thus ab = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² + 2 and g(x) = 2x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(2)) = 51, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−11","7","3","49"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(2) = 4 + k, so f(g(2)) = (4 + k)² + 2 = 51. Then (4 + k)² = 49, so 4 + k = 7 or 4 + k = −7. These give k = 3 or k = −11. Since k is positive, k = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 8x + 15. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x + 6). What is the product of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["15","3","99","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 3)(x − 5), so the zeros of f are 3 and 5. g(x) = 0 when x + 6 equals a zero of f, so x = 3 − 6 = −3 or x = 5 − 6 = −1. The product is 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 2<sup>x + 2</sup> − 2<sup>x − 2</sup> = 30, what is the value of 4<sup>x</sup>?$q$, $q$["64","3","8","32"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 2<sup>x − 2</sup>: 2<sup>x − 2</sup>(2<sup>4</sup> − 1) = 30, so 2<sup>x − 2</sup>(15) = 30 and 2<sup>x − 2</sup> = 2. Then x − 2 = 1, so x = 3, and 4<sup>x</sup> = 64.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$2x² + bx + c ≤ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is −7 ≤ x ≤ −4. What is the value of b?$q$, $q$["−22","−11","11","22"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are −7 and −4, and the expression equals 2(x + 7)(x + 4) (the parabola opens upward, so it is ≤ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives 2x² + 22x + 56, so b = 22 and c = 56.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The number of cells in a culture doubles every 16 hours. At time t = 0, there are 1200 cells. Which function gives the number of cells, N(t), t minutes after time t = 0?$q$, $q$["1200(2)<sup>t/16</sup>","1200(2)<sup>960t</sup>","1200(2)<sup>t/960</sup>","1200(2)<sup>16t</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The quantity is multiplied by 2 once every 16 hours. Since t is measured in minutes, 16 hours is 960 minutes, so the number of periods after t minutes is t/960. Therefore N(t) = 1200(2)<sup>t/960</sup>. Using t/16 would treat t as hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −x² + 6x + 9<br>y = 14x + 21<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁x₂?$q$, $q$["−12","12","−9","−21"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: −x² + 6x + 9 = 14x + 21, so −x² − 8x − 12 = 0, which factors as −(x + 6)(x + 2) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are −6 and −2, with product 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x + 9)(4x − 7) − 2(x² + 1) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["−34","−30","−26","34"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (10)(−3) − 2(1 + 1) = −30 − 4 = −34. (Expanding fully gives 2x² + 29x − 65.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = −x² − 9x − 8<br>y = −x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−8","7","12","8"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: −x² − 9x − 8 = −x + k, so −x² − 8x − 8 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (−8)² − 4(−1)(−8 − k) = 0, which gives k = 8. The repeated solution is x = −(−8)/(2·(−1)) = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(2x + 28) = x + 2<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−6","−2","4","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 2x + 28 = x² + 4x + 4, so x² + 2x − 24 = 0, which factors as (x − 4)(x + 6) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 2 must be nonnegative. For x = −6, the right side is −4 < 0, so x = −6 is extraneous. Only x = 4 works, so the sum of all solutions is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$2<sup>2x</sup> − 12(2<sup>x</sup>) + 32 = 0<br><br>What is the greatest solution to the given equation?$q$, $q$["12","3","5","8"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 2<sup>x</sup>, so 2<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 12u + 32 = 0, which factors as (u − 4)(u − 8) = 0. So 2<sup>x</sup> = 4 or 2<sup>x</sup> = 8, giving x = 2 or x = 3. The greatest solution is 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + 5x² + bx + c<br><br>In the given polynomial function, b and c are constants. If −5 and 2 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["−2","2","0","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c, the sum of the zeros is −a. Here −a = −5, so −5 + 2 + r = −5, giving r = −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 64t + 82. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 130 feet?$q$, $q$["3","1","2.5","2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 130: −16t² + 64t + 82 = 130, so −16t² + 64t − 48 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 4t + 3 = 0, so t = 1 or t = 3. The object is at or above 130 feet between these times, for 3 − 1 = 2 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The value of an investment increases by 125% every 2 years. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per year, by what percent does it increase each year?$q$, $q$["62.5%","125%","50%","31%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-year growth factor is r, then r<sup>2</sup> = 1 + 1.25 = 2.25. Taking the square root gives r = 1.5, which is a 50% increase each year. Dividing 125% by 2 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(3) = 432 and f(6) = 3456, what is the value of f(8)?$q$, $q$["5472","13824","6912","27648"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(6)/f(3) = b<sup>3</sup> = 8, so b = 2. Then a = f(3)/b<sup>3</sup> = 54, so f(x) = 54(2)<sup>x</sup> and f(8) = 54(2)<sup>8</sup> = 13824.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 8x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 2. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["15","−15","16","17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to 8 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 2. So they are (8 + 2)/2 = 5 and (8 − 2)/2 = 3. Their product is c = (5)(3) = 15.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² + kx + 16 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant. For how many positive integer values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["15","8","6","7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant is negative: k² − 4(1)(16) < 0, so k² < 64. The positive integers with k² < 64 are 1 through 7, so there are 7 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 3, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to ∜(x<sup>6</sup>) · √(x), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>1</sup>","x<sup>3/4</sup>","x<sup>2</sup>","x<sup>7/6</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: ∜(x<sup>6</sup>) = x<sup>3/2</sup> and √(x) = x<sup>1/2</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 3/2 + 1/2 = 2, so the expression equals x<sup>2</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (x² + 6x − 11)/(x − 1) is equivalent to x + 7 + R/(x − 1), where R is a constant and x ≠ 1. What is the value of R?$q$, $q$["−11","−4","4","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiply both sides by (x − 1): x² + 6x − 11 = (x − 1)(x + 7) + R = x² + 6x − 7 + R. Matching constants: −11 = −7 + R, so R = −4. (Equivalently, by the remainder theorem, R equals the numerator evaluated at x = 1.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = 3. If f(0) = 47 and f(−1) = 68, what is the value of f(8)?$q$, $q$["95","20","98","−95"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − 3)² + k. From f(0): a(9) + k = 47. From f(−1): a(16) + k = 68. Subtracting gives −7a = −21, so a = 3 and k = 20. Then f(8) = 3(5)² + 20 = 95.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = 3x³ + ax² + 3x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x − 2, the remainder is 37, and p(3) = 102. What is the value of a + b?$q$, $q$["3","−4","1","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(2) = 37: 24 + 4a + 6 + b = 37, so 4a + b = 7. Also p(3) = 102: 9a + b = 12. Subtracting, −5a = −5, so a = 1 and b = 3. Thus a + b = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² − 5 and g(x) = 3x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(1)) = 76, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−12","9","6","81"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(1) = 3 + k, so f(g(1)) = (3 + k)² − 5 = 76. Then (3 + k)² = 81, so 3 + k = 9 or 3 + k = −9. These give k = 6 or k = −12. Since k is positive, k = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − x − 42. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x − 5). What is the product of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["−42","−12","−22","11"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x − 7)(x + 6), so the zeros of f are 7 and −6. g(x) = 0 when x − 5 equals a zero of f, so x = 7 + 5 = 12 or x = −6 + 5 = −1. The product is −12.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 3<sup>x + 2</sup> − 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 2106, what is the value of 3<sup>x + 1</sup>?$q$, $q$["729","5","243","487"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 3<sup>x − 1</sup>: 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(3<sup>3</sup> − 1) = 2106, so 3<sup>x − 1</sup>(26) = 2106 and 3<sup>x − 1</sup> = 81. Then x − 1 = 4, so x = 5, and 3<sup>x + 1</sup> = 729.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$3x² + bx + c ≤ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is 5 ≤ x ≤ 10. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["−150","50","153","150"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are 5 and 10, and the expression equals 3(x − 5)(x − 10) (the parabola opens upward, so it is ≤ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives 3x² − 45x + 150, so b = −45 and c = 150.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = x² + 3x − 4<br>y = 8x + 2<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of y₁ + y₂?$q$, $q$["5","44","−56","−44"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: x² + 3x − 4 = 8x + 2, so x² − 5x − 6 = 0, which factors as (x + 1)(x − 6) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are −1 and 6, and the corresponding y-values on the line are 8(−1) + 2 = −6 and 8(6) + 2 = 50. The sum is 44.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (−2x − 3)(−2x − 1) − 2(x² − 5) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["23","3","15","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (−5)(−3) − 2(1 − 5) = 15 + 8 = 23. (Expanding fully gives 2x² + 8x + 13.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h41$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 3x² + 5x − 5<br>y = −x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−5","8","−7","−8"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: 3x² + 5x − 5 = −x + k, so 3x² + 6x − 5 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (6)² − 4(3)(−5 − k) = 0, which gives k = −8. The repeated solution is x = −(6)/(2·3) = −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h42$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$√(3x + 72) = x + 6<br><br>What is the sum of all solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["−12","−9","3","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Squaring both sides gives 3x + 72 = x² + 12x + 36, so x² + 9x − 36 = 0, which factors as (x − 3)(x + 12) = 0. Squaring can introduce extraneous solutions, so check both: the right side x + 6 must be nonnegative. For x = −12, the right side is −6 < 0, so x = −12 is extraneous. Only x = 3 works, so the sum of all solutions is 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h43$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$2<sup>2x</sup> − 20(2<sup>x</sup>) + 64 = 0<br><br>What is the sum of the solutions to the given equation?$q$, $q$["20","6","8","16"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let u = 2<sup>x</sup>, so 2<sup>2x</sup> = u². The equation becomes u² − 20u + 64 = 0, which factors as (u − 4)(u − 16) = 0. So 2<sup>x</sup> = 4 or 2<sup>x</sup> = 16, giving x = 2 or x = 4. The sum is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h44$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, null, $q$p(x) = x³ + 15x² + bx + c<br><br>In the given polynomial function, b and c are constants. If −4 and −5 are zeros of p, what is the third zero of p?$q$, $q$["−6","6","−4","−9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For a cubic x³ + ax² + bx + c, the sum of the zeros is −a. Here −a = −15, so −4 + (−5) + r = −15, giving r = −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h45$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$An object is launched upward from a platform. Its height h, in feet, t seconds after launch is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 56t + 110. For how many seconds is the object at a height of at least 150 feet?$q$, $q$["2.5","1","1.75","1.5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Set h(t) = 150: −16t² + 56t + 110 = 150, so −16t² + 56t − 40 = 0. Dividing by −16 gives t² − 3.5t + 2.5 = 0, so t = 1 or t = 2.5. The object is at or above 150 feet between these times, for 2.5 − 1 = 1.5 seconds.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h46$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The value of an investment increases by 96% every 2 weeks. Assuming the increase occurs at a constant percent rate per week, by what percent does it increase each week?$q$, $q$["48%","96%","40%","24%"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$If the per-week growth factor is r, then r<sup>2</sup> = 1 + 0.96 = 1.96. Taking the square root gives r = 1.4, which is a 40% increase each week. Dividing 96% by 2 is a common error, because percent changes compound rather than add.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h47$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = ab<sup>x</sup>, where a and b are positive constants. If f(2) = 36 and f(4) = 324, what is the value of f(5)?$q$, $q$["468","972","324","2916"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Dividing, f(4)/f(2) = b<sup>2</sup> = 9, so b = 3. Then a = f(2)/b<sup>2</sup> = 4, so f(x) = 4(3)<sup>x</sup> and f(5) = 4(3)<sup>5</sup> = 972.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h48$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$x² − 7x + c = 0<br><br>In the given equation, c is a constant. The equation has two real solutions, and the positive difference between them is 7. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["0","7","1","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The solutions add to 7 (the opposite of the x-coefficient) and differ by 7. So they are (7 + 7)/2 = 7 and (7 − 7)/2 = 0. Their product is c = (7)(0) = 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h49$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, null, $q$3x² + kx + 6 = 0<br><br>In the given equation, k is an integer constant. For how many values of k does the equation have no real solutions?$q$, $q$["8","19","16","17"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$No real solutions means the discriminant is negative: k² − 4(3)(6) < 0, so k² < 72. The integers satisfying this are −8 through 8, including 0, which is 17 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h50$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 4, null, $q$Which expression is equivalent to ∛(x<sup>5</sup>) · ∜(x<sup>3</sup>), where x > 0?$q$, $q$["x<sup>11/12</sup>","x<sup>5/4</sup>","x<sup>29/12</sup>","x<sup>8/7</sup>"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Rewrite each radical as a rational exponent: ∛(x<sup>5</sup>) = x<sup>5/3</sup> and ∜(x<sup>3</sup>) = x<sup>3/4</sup>. Multiplying powers with the same base adds exponents: 5/3 + 3/4 = 29/12, so the expression equals x<sup>29/12</sup>.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h51$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$(x² + kx − 6)/(x − 1) = x + 6<br><br>In the given equation, k is a constant, and the equation is true for all x ≠ 1. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−7","5","7","−6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Multiplying both sides by x − 1 gives x² + kx − 6 = (x − 1)(x + 6) = x² + 5x − 6. Matching x-coefficients gives k = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h52$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is a quadratic function whose graph in the xy-plane has its vertex at x = −6. If f(0) = −92 and f(−10) = −52, what is the value of f(−9)?$q$, $q$["−38","−20","−40","38"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Write f(x) = a(x − (−6))² + k. From f(0): a(36) + k = −92. From f(−10): a(16) + k = −52. Subtracting gives 20a = −40, so a = −2 and k = −20. Then f(−9) = −2(−3)² − 20 = −38.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h53$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 4, null, $q$p(x) = 2x³ + ax² + 2x + b<br><br>In the polynomial p, a and b are constants. When p(x) is divided by x + 2, the remainder is −18, and p(−1) = −11. What is the value of a + b?$q$, $q$["−30","7","3","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$By the remainder theorem, p(−2) = −18: −16 + 4a − 4 + b = −18, so 4a + b = 2. Also p(−1) = −11: a + b = −7. Subtracting, 3a = 9, so a = 3 and b = −10. Thus a + b = −7.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h54$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 3, null, $q$The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = x² + 6 and g(x) = 2x + k, where k is a positive constant. If f(g(3)) = 106, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−16","10","4","100"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(3) = 6 + k, so f(g(3)) = (6 + k)² + 6 = 106. Then (6 + k)² = 100, so 6 + k = 10 or 6 + k = −10. These give k = 4 or k = −16. Since k is positive, k = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h55$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, null, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 5x − 24. The function g is defined by g(x) = f(x − 2). What is the sum of the zeros of g?$q$, $q$["5","9","1","−9"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$f(x) = (x + 3)(x − 8), so the zeros of f are −3 and 8. g(x) = 0 when x − 2 equals a zero of f, so x = −3 + 2 = −1 or x = 8 + 2 = 10. The sum is 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h56$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 4, null, $q$If 2<sup>x + 1</sup> − 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 6, what is the value of 4<sup>x</sup>?$q$, $q$["16","2","4","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Factor out the smaller power, 2<sup>x − 1</sup>: 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(2<sup>2</sup> − 1) = 6, so 2<sup>x − 1</sup>(3) = 6 and 2<sup>x − 1</sup> = 2. Then x − 1 = 1, so x = 2, and 4<sup>x</sup> = 16.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h57$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 4, null, $q$−x² + bx + c ≥ 0<br><br>In the given inequality, b and c are constants. The solution set of the inequality is −1 ≤ x ≤ 3. What is the value of c?$q$, $q$["−3","2","4","3"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The solution set is the interval between the zeros, so the zeros are −1 and 3, and the expression equals −1(x + 1)(x − 3) (the parabola opens downward, so it is ≥ 0 between its zeros). Expanding gives −x² + 2x + 3, so b = 2 and c = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h59$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = 2x² + 6x − 2<br>y = 10x + 28<br><br>The graphs of the given equations in the xy-plane intersect at the points (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂). What is the value of x₁x₂?$q$, $q$["15","−15","−1","14"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Set the expressions equal: 2x² + 6x − 2 = 10x + 28, so 2x² − 4x − 30 = 0, which factors as 2(x − 5)(x + 3) = 0. So x₁ and x₂ are 5 and −3, with product −15.$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h60$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 3, null, $q$The expression (−4x + 8)(−4x − 5) − 3(x² + 3) is equivalent to ax² + bx + c, where a, b, and c are constants. What is the value of a + b + c?$q$, $q$["−48","−30","−36","−24"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A quick route: a + b + c is the value of the expression at x = 1. That gives (4)(−9) − 3(1 + 3) = −36 − 12 = −48. (Expanding fully gives 13x² − 12x − 49.)$q$, null),
  ($q$adv-h61$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, null, $q$y = x² − 2x + 2<br>y = 6x + k<br><br>In the given system of equations, k is a constant. If the system has exactly one real solution, what is the value of x in that solution?$q$, $q$["−4","8","5","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Setting the expressions equal: x² − 2x + 2 = 6x + k, so x² − 8x + 2 − k = 0. Exactly one solution means the discriminant is 0: (−8)² − 4(1)(2 − k) = 0, which gives k = −14. The repeated solution is x = −(−8)/(2·1) = 4.$q$, null)
